;; clojure.edn/read-string -- restated over `:document`, the one value type
;; of this language that carries arbitrary nested EDN, which is exactly what
;; clojure.edn/read-string answers when the text is EDN. Under the name
;; `kotoba.edn/read-document`, because the language reserves `read-string`
;; (see WHY THE NAME).
;;
;; WHY THIS EXISTS (adr-2609111900, the self-host ceiling loop, 2026-09-12):
;; at selfhost-distance measurement 19 (amu cdebca89, 225 compiler sources
;; on the project route) 11 modules were refused
;;   `required module clojure.edn is missing from the explicit source paths`
;; -- a wall, not a defect: nothing on the guest route answered to the name.
;; Reader survey of the corpus the same day: 17 of the 225 files require
;; clojure.edn (`[clojure.edn :as edn]` 14, `:as reader` 1 inside a :clj
;; branch, 2 fully qualified); call sites per name: edn/read-string 33, every
;; one 1-arity on a string; edn/read 4 (2 with `{:eof eof}` and a
;; PushbackReader in bounded_edn, 1 on a reader in grade_a) plus 3 qualified
;; `clojure.edn/read` on a `java.io.PushbackReader` in kotoba-sema's own
;; frontend; `edn/read-all` 1, in a comment (it is kotoba.lang.edn's name,
;; not clojure.edn's).
;;
;; WHY THE NAME. `(defn read-string [s :string] :document ...)` is refused
;; `reserved function name` (:kotoba.error/subset-reject) by kotoba-sema --
;; measured 2026-09-12 through `amu check --jvm-free` at amu 5d8e23e4, and
;; again on kotoba-lang/edn's own src/kotoba/lang/canonical_document.kotoba
;; (written 2026-08-03, refused at its line 20 today). `read-string` is in
;; lang/guest-grammar.edn :forbidden-heads, the set that refuses
;; `clojure.core/read-string` -- the host reader that admits reader eval and
;; mints host objects from tagged literals -- and kotoba-sema's
;; reserved-function-names is that set plus the builtin heads: a head is a
;; bare symbol there, so the SAFE reader's name collides with the unsafe
;; one, the way `ref` did (lang/surface-status.edn :spelling-collision).
;; No guest module can carry the name until the grammar and the frontend
;; tell the two apart. So the function lives here under a DIFFERENT name in
;; a `kotoba.*` namespace, saying what it answers (a document from EDN
;; text), as lang/compat.edn's rule for every name that cannot be provided
;; under its Clojure spelling -- and `:modules :clojure.edn` records
;; read-string ABSENT for exactly this reason, pointing here. A caller
;; changes `(edn/read-string s)` to `(edn/read-document s)` with
;; `(:require [kotoba.edn :as edn])`; when the name is admitted,
;; clojure.edn/read-string becomes a one-line forwarder to this.
;;
;; WHAT IS PROVIDED: `read-document`, clojure.edn/read-string's 1-arity over
;; a string, answering a `:document`. The reader is the language's own
;; `document-edn-read` (kotoba.kir.value, an inert parser: no tagged
;; literal, no discard, no reader eval can run), and clojure.edn/read-string
;; is the oracle: test/kotoba/lang/clojure_edn_compat_test.cljk runs this
;; text on the KIR interpreter over 55 EDN texts and compares values and
;; container shapes with the host reader. NOT `document-read`: that is the
;; inverse of `document-print`, the lowercase-hex canonical-bytes codec --
;; `(document-read "{:a 1}")` traps `document-read hex must be lowercase
;; [0-9a-f]`, measured 2026-09-12.
;;
;; WHERE THE HOST READS SOMETHING ELSE, THIS REFUSES. `document-edn-read`
;; alone is not clojure.edn: it reads `1/2`, `0x10`, `2r101`, `123N`, `1.5M`,
;; `\a`, `1e`, `1_000` as SYMBOLS where the host reads a ratio, an integer,
;; a bigint, a bigdec, a character, or refuses; `010` as 10 where the host
;; reads octal 8; `.5` as 0.5 where the host reads a symbol; `::k`, `a::b`,
;; `k/`, `:k/`, `a:` as keywords and symbols where the host refuses them;
;; a form feed or U+2028 as token text where the host takes whitespace; and
;; `^:m x` as a symbol `^:m` followed by a trailing form where the host
;; answers `x`. Every one of those is a different ANSWER on an input both
;; accept, which a twin may not give. So `read-document` scans the text
;; first (`text-admitted?`, one iterative loop over the bytes, skipping
;; strings and comments) and TRAPS on any token of those shapes -- `assert`,
;; which is :division-by-zero: the portable trap ABI has no message channel
;; -- before `document-edn-read` sees it. The admitted number shape is
;; `[+-]? digits ("." digits*)? ([eE] [+-]? digits+)?` with an integer not
;; written with a leading zero (`00` and `007` are refused too, where the
;; host answers 0 and 7: a leading zero is octal there, and refusing the
;; whole shape is simpler than reading octal). `'x` is NOT refused: the EDN
;; reader has no quote macro and answers the symbol `'x`, as this does.
;;
;; WHERE THE HOST ANSWERS AND THIS TRAPS BY NAME (the reader's own limits,
;; each asserted in the oracle test):
;;   * the empty string, whitespace only, a comment only: `document-edn-read
;;     unexpected end of input`. clojure.edn answers nil. (kotoba.lang.edn
;;     refuses too, `EDN input is empty`.)
;;   * two top-level forms: `document-edn-read trailing forms`. clojure.edn
;;     answers the first and never looks at the second.
;;   * `#inst`, `#uuid`, any tagged literal, `#_` discard, `#:ns{}`:
;;     `document-edn-read dispatch forms are forbidden`. The reader admits
;;     one dispatch prefix, `#{`.
;;   * an integer outside i64: `invalid document i64` on this host (the JVM
;;     interpreter says `document-edn-read i64 out of range`); the host
;;     reads a BigInt.
;;   * a container at depth 9: `document-edn-read depth limit exceeded`; a
;;     33rd item: `... item limit exceeded`; a text over 65536 bytes cannot
;;     be a string value at all. The host has no limits.
;;   * a raw newline inside a string: `document-edn-read newline in string`;
;;     an octal escape `"\101"`: `... unsupported string escape`. The host
;;     answers both.
;;   * a duplicate map key or set item: `document-edn-read duplicate map
;;     key` / `duplicate set item`, where the host throws too.
;;
;; WHERE THE TWO DIFFER IN TYPE, AND ON PURPOSE: the parameter is `:string`
;; and the result `:document`. `(edn/read-document 42)` is a CHECK-TIME
;; refusal at the call site (`expression type mismatch: expected string,
;; got i64`), not a different answer.
;;
;; DEPTH. Nothing here recurses: the scan is one `loop`, the reader is the
;; host's, so the KIR reference interpreter on Node's default stack answers
;; every depth the document limit allows in-process (the oracle test needs
;; no child Node, unlike clojure.walk's).
;;
;; Consumed through the ordinary project route, which needs no JVM:
;;   amu check app.kotoba --source-path <app> --source-path <this repo>/lang/compat --jvm-free

(ns kotoba.edn
  (:export [read-document]))

;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
;; bytes and code points

;; UTF-8 width from the code point itself; advancing by it keeps every offset
;; on a code-point boundary (`string-code-point-at` REFUSES an interior byte).
(defn- code-point-width [point :i64] :i64
  (if (< point 128)
    1
    (if (< point 2048)
      2
      (if (< point 65536) 3 4))))

;; The code point at byte offset `i`, or -1 at or past `end`.
(defn- point-at [s :string i :i64 end :i64] :i64
  (if (< i end) (string-code-point-at s i) -1))

(defn- digit? [p :i64] :bool
  (and (>= p 48) (<= p 57)))

(defn- sign? [p :i64] :bool
  (or (= p 43) (= p 45)))

;; What ends a token for `document-edn-read`: its five whitespace bytes
;; (space, tab, newline, return, comma) and the eight structural bytes
;; `[ ] { } ( ) " ;`.
(defn- delimiter? [p :i64] :bool
  (or (= p 32) (= p 9) (= p 10) (= p 13) (= p 44)
      (= p 91) (= p 93) (= p 123) (= p 125) (= p 40) (= p 41) (= p 34) (= p 59)))

;; Whitespace to the host reader (java.lang.Character/isWhitespace) that
;; `document-edn-read` takes as token text: the JVM's whitespace minus the
;; four bytes both agree on (9, 10, 13, 32). The code points are the ones
;; kotoba.string's `whitespace?` lists, measured there against the JVM.
(defn- host-only-whitespace? [p :i64] :bool
  (cond
    (< p 11) false
    (<= p 12) true          ; U+000B, U+000C
    (< p 28) false
    (<= p 31) true          ; U+001C..U+001F
    (= p 5760) true         ; U+1680 OGHAM SPACE MARK
    (< p 8192) false
    (<= p 8198) true        ; U+2000..U+2006
    (= p 8199) false        ; U+2007 FIGURE SPACE, non-breaking
    (<= p 8202) true        ; U+2008..U+200A
    (= p 8232) true         ; U+2028 LINE SEPARATOR
    (= p 8233) true         ; U+2029 PARAGRAPH SEPARATOR
    (= p 8287) true         ; U+205F MEDIUM MATHEMATICAL SPACE
    (= p 12288) true        ; U+3000 IDEOGRAPHIC SPACE
    :else false))

;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
;; one token

;; The byte offset just past the token that starts at `i`.
(defn- token-end [s :string i :i64 end :i64] :i64
  (loop [j i]
    (if (< j end)
      (let [p (string-code-point-at s j)]
        (if (delimiter? p) j (recur (+ j (code-point-width p)))))
      j)))

;; The offset just past the run of ASCII digits starting at `i`.
(defn- digits-end [s :string i :i64 end :i64] :i64
  (loop [j i]
    (if (digit? (point-at s j end)) (recur (+ j 1)) j)))

;; Whether the token at [start, end), known to begin `[+-]?[0-9]`, has the
;; ONE number shape both readers read the same way:
;;   [+-]? digits ( "." digits* )? ( [eE] [+-]? digits+ )?
;; with an integer (no fraction, no exponent) not written with a leading
;; zero -- the host reads `010` as octal 8 and `08` as an invalid number
;; where `document-edn-read` reads 10 and 8. Everything else the host reads
;; as a number and this reader as a symbol (`1/2` `0x10` `2r101` `123N`
;; `1.5M`) or the host refuses (`1e` `1_000` `1..2`) is false here.
(defn- number-shape-ok? [s :string start :i64 end :i64] :bool
  (let [i0 (if (sign? (point-at s start end)) (+ start 1) start)
        i1 (digits-end s i0 end)
        after-fraction (if (= 46 (point-at s i1 end)) (digits-end s (+ i1 1) end) i1)
        exp-marker (point-at s after-fraction end)
        after-exponent (if (or (= exp-marker 101) (= exp-marker 69))
                         (let [k (if (sign? (point-at s (+ after-fraction 1) end))
                                   (+ after-fraction 2)
                                   (+ after-fraction 1))
                               k-end (digits-end s k end)]
                           (if (= k-end k) -1 k-end))
                         after-fraction)
        integer? (= after-exponent i1)
        leading-zero-run? (and (= 48 (point-at s i0 end)) (> (- i1 i0) 1))]
    (and (= after-exponent end)
         (not (and integer? leading-zero-run?)))))

;; Whether a symbol or keyword token at [start, end) is one the host reads
;; at all: no `::` inside it, not ending in `:`, not ending in `/` unless it
;; IS `/`, and no host-only whitespace inside it (which the host would have
;; taken as the token's end).
(defn- name-shape-ok? [s :string start :i64 end :i64] :bool
  (loop [j start prev -1]
    (if (< j end)
      (let [p (string-code-point-at s j)]
        (if (or (host-only-whitespace? p) (and (= p 58) (= prev 58)))
          false
          (recur (+ j (code-point-width p)) p)))
      (not (or (= prev 58)
               (and (= prev 47) (> (- end start) 1)))))))

;; Whether the token at [start, end) is one `document-edn-read` answers the
;; way the host does. False is a refusal: the host and this reader would
;; answer DIFFERENT values, or the host would refuse.
(defn- token-admitted? [s :string start :i64 end :i64] :bool
  (let [p0 (point-at s start end)
        p1 (point-at s (+ start (code-point-width p0)) end)
        p2 (point-at s (+ start (+ (code-point-width p0) (code-point-width p1))) end)]
    (cond
      (= p0 92) false                                   ; \c -- a host character
      (or (= p0 96) (= p0 126) (= p0 64) (= p0 94)) false ; ` ~ @ refused by the host; ^ is metadata there
      (and (= p0 58) (= p1 58)) false                   ; ::k -- an invalid token on the host
      (digit? p0) (number-shape-ok? s start end)
      (and (sign? p0) (digit? p1)) (number-shape-ok? s start end)
      (and (= p0 46) (digit? p1)) false                 ; .5 -- a SYMBOL on the host
      (and (sign? p0) (= p1 46) (digit? p2)) false     ; +.5 -- a symbol on the host
      :else (name-shape-ok? s start end))))

;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
;; the text

;; Whether every token of `s` outside strings and comments is admitted.
;; Iterative (one loop, mode 0 code / 1 string / 2 comment / 3 the code
;; point after a backslash in a string), so the host stack is never the
;; ceiling. A `#` is stepped over: `#{` is a set and
;; anything else is refused by `document-edn-read` itself, by name.
(defn- text-admitted? [s :string] :bool
  (let [end (string-byte-length s)]
    (loop [i 0 mode 0]
      (if (< i end)
        (let [p (string-code-point-at s i)]
          (cond
            (= mode 3) (recur (+ i (code-point-width p)) 1)
            (= mode 1) (recur (+ i (code-point-width p)) (if (= p 92) 3 (if (= p 34) 0 1)))
            (= mode 2) (recur (+ i (code-point-width p)) (if (= p 10) 0 2))
            (= p 34) (recur (+ i 1) 1)
            (= p 59) (recur (+ i 1) 2)
            (or (delimiter? p) (= p 35)) (recur (+ i 1) 0)
            :else (let [e (token-end s i end)]
                    (if (token-admitted? s i e) (recur e 0) false))))
        true))))

;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
;; the export

(defn read-document [s :string] :document
  (do (assert (text-admitted? s))
      (document-edn-read s)))
